#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:2
msgid "General"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:5
msgid "What is Syncthing?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:7
msgid ""
"Syncthing is an application that lets you synchronize your files across "
"multiple devices. This means the creation, modification or deletion of files "
"on one machine will automatically be replicated to your other devices. We "
"believe your data is your data alone and you deserve to choose where it is "
"stored. Therefore Syncthing does not upload your data to the cloud but "
"exchanges your data across your machines as soon as they are online at the "
"same time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:15
msgid "Is it \"syncthing\", \"Syncthing\" or \"SyncThing\"?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:17
msgid ""
"It's **Syncthing**, although the command and source repository is spelled "
"``syncthing`` so it may be referred to in that way as well. It's definitely "
"not :strike:`SyncThing`, even though the abbreviation ``st`` is used in some "
"circumstances and file names."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:23
msgid "What things are synced?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:25
msgid "The following things are *always* synchronized:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:27
msgid "File contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:28
msgid "File modification times"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:30
msgid "The following may be synchronized or not, depending:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:32
msgid ""
"File permissions (when supported by file system; on Windows only the read "
"only bit is synchronized)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:34
msgid "Symbolic links (synced, except on Windows, but never followed)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:35
msgid "File or directory owners and groups (when enabled)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:36
msgid "Extended attributes (when enabled)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:37
msgid "POSIX or NFS ACLs (as part of extended attributes)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:41
msgid ""
"Note that what can be synced often depends on the operating system, file "
"system, and possibly other factors. For example, even though Syncthing may "
"try to synchronise symbolic links on Android, this will not succeed, as the "
"OS does not support them on the user storage."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:46
msgid "The following are *not* synchronized;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:48
msgid "Directory modification times (not preserved)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:49
msgid "Hard links (followed, not preserved)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:50
msgid ""
"Windows junctions (synced as ordinary directories; require enabling in :"
"stconf:opt:`the configuration <folder.junctionsAsDirs>` on a per-folder "
"basis)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:53
msgid "Resource forks (not preserved)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:54
msgid "Windows ACLs (not preserved)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:55
msgid "Devices, FIFOs, and other specials (ignored)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:56
msgid ""
"Sparse file sparseness (will become sparse, when supported by the OS & "
"filesystem)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:57
msgid ""
"Syncthing internal files and folders (e.g. ``.stfolder``, ``.stignore``, ``."
"stversions``, :ref:`temporary files <temporary-files>`, etc.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:61
msgid "Is synchronization fast?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:63
msgid ""
"Syncthing segments files into pieces, called blocks, to transfer data from "
"one device to another. Therefore, multiple devices can share the "
"synchronization load, in a similar way to the torrent protocol. The more "
"devices you have online, the faster an additional device will receive the "
"data because small blocks will be fetched from all devices in parallel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:69
msgid ""
"Syncthing handles renaming files and updating their metadata in an efficient "
"manner. This means that renaming a file will not cause a retransmission of "
"that file. Additionally, appending data to existing files should be handled "
"efficiently as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:74
msgid ""
":ref:`Temporary files <temporary-files>` are used to store partial data "
"downloaded from other devices. They are automatically removed whenever a "
"file transfer has been completed or after the configured amount of time "
"which is set in the configuration file (24 hours by default)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:80
msgid "How does Syncthing differ from BitTorrent/Resilio Sync?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:82
msgid ""
"The two are different and not related. Syncthing and BitTorrent/Resilio Sync "
"accomplish some of the same things, namely syncing files between two or more "
"computers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:85
msgid ""
"BitTorrent Sync, now called Resilio Sync, is a proprietary peer-to-peer file "
"synchronization tool available for Windows, Mac, Linux, Android, iOS, "
"Windows Phone, Amazon Kindle Fire and BSD. [#resiliosync]_ Syncthing is an "
"open source file synchronization tool."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:90
msgid ""
"Syncthing uses an open and documented protocol, and likewise the security "
"mechanisms in use are well defined and visible in the source code. Resilio "
"Sync uses an undocumented, closed protocol with unknown security properties."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:94
msgid "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Resilio_Sync"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:97
msgid "Is there an iOS client?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:99
msgid ""
"There are no plans by the current Syncthing team to officially support iOS "
"in the foreseeable future."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:101
msgid ""
"iOS has significant restrictions on background processing that make it very "
"hard to run Syncthing reliably and integrate it into the system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:104
msgid ""
"However, there is an open source app for iOS, incorporating Syncthing, that "
"attempts to work within these limitations. It provides a native UI and "
"features for selective synchronization as well as on-demand access to files. "
"Most Syncthing features are available, but the native UI is simplified "
"compared to the official client. [#synctrain]_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:109
msgid ""
"There is also a commercial packaging of Syncthing. It provides access to all "
"Syncthing functionalities through the original UI. [#mobiussync]_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:112
msgid "https://github.com/pixelspark/sushitrain"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:113
msgid "https://www.mobiussync.com"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:116
msgid "Should I keep my device IDs secret?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:118
msgid ""
"No. The IDs are not sensitive. Given a device ID it's possible to find the "
"IP address for that device, if global discovery is enabled on it. Knowing "
"the device ID doesn't help you actually establish a connection to that "
"device or get a list of files, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:123
msgid ""
"For a connection to be established, both devices need to know about the "
"other's device ID. It's not possible (in practice) to forge a device ID. (To "
"forge a device ID you need to create a TLS certificate with that specific "
"SHA-256 hash. If you can do that, you can spoof any TLS certificate. The "
"world is your oyster!)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/general.rst:130
msgid ":ref:`device-ids`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:2
msgid "Troubleshooting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:5
msgid "Where are the Syncthing logs?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:7
msgid ""
"Syncthing logs to stdout by default. On Windows Syncthing by default also "
"creates ``syncthing.log`` in Syncthing's home directory (run ``syncthing --"
"paths`` to see where that is). The command line option ``--logfile`` can be "
"used to specify a user-defined logfile.  If you only have access to a "
"running instance's GUI, click the `Help` -> `About` menu item, then the "
"`Paths` tab."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:13
msgid ""
"If you're running a process manager like systemd, check there. If you're "
"using a GUI wrapper integration, it may keep the logs for you."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:17
msgid "Why is the sync so slow?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:19
msgid ""
"When troubleshooting a slow sync, there are a number of things to check."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:21
msgid ""
"First of all, verify that you are not connected via a relay. In the \"Remote "
"Devices\" list on the right side of the GUI, double check that the "
"Connection Type does *not* start with \"Relay\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:25
msgid "Here is an example of a device connected via a relay:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:29
msgid ""
"If you are connected via a relay, this is because a direct connection could "
"not be established. Double check and follow the suggestions in :ref:"
"`firewall-setup` to enable direct connections."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:33
msgid ""
"Second, if one of the devices is a very low powered machine (a Raspberry Pi, "
"or a phone, or a NAS, or similar) you are likely constrained by the CPU on "
"that device. See the next question for reasons Syncthing likes a faster CPU."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:37
msgid ""
"Third, verify that the network connection is OK. Tools such as iperf or just "
"an Internet speed test can be used to verify the performance here."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:41
msgid "Why does it use so much CPU?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:43
msgid ""
"When new or changed files are detected, or Syncthing starts for the first "
"time, your files are hashed using SHA-256."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:46
msgid ""
"Data that is sent over the network is compressed (optionally) and encrypted "
"(always). When receiving data it must be decrypted and then (if compressed) "
"decompressed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:50
msgid ""
"There is a certain amount of housekeeping that must be done to track the "
"current and available versions of each file in the index database."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:53
msgid ""
"By default Syncthing uses periodic scanning every hour when watching for "
"changes or every minute if that's disabled to detect file changes. This "
"means checking every file's modification time and comparing it to the "
"database. This can cause spikes of CPU usage for large folders."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:59
msgid ""
"Hashing, compression and encryption cost CPU time. Also, using the GUI "
"causes a certain amount of extra CPU usage to calculate the summary data it "
"presents. Note however that once things are *in sync* CPU usage should be "
"negligible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:64
msgid ""
"To minimize the impact of this, Syncthing attempts to :stconf:opt:`lower the "
"process priority <setLowPriority>` when starting up."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:67
msgid ""
"To further limit the amount of CPU used when syncing and scanning, set the "
"environment variable ``GOMAXPROCS`` to the maximum number of CPU cores "
"Syncthing should use at any given moment. For example, ``GOMAXPROCS=2`` on a "
"machine with four cores will limit Syncthing to no more than half the "
"system's CPU power."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:74
msgid "Why is the setup more complicated than BitTorrent/Resilio Sync?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:76
msgid ""
"Security over convenience. In Syncthing you have to set up both sides to "
"connect two devices. An attacker can't do much with a stolen device ID, "
"because you have to add the device on the other side too. You have better "
"control where your files are transferred."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:81
msgid "This is an area that we are working to improve in the long term."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:84
msgid "Why do I get \"Host check error\" in the GUI/API?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:86
msgid ""
"Since version 0.14.6 Syncthing does an extra security check when the GUI/API "
"is bound to localhost - namely that the browser is talking to localhost. "
"This protects against most forms of `DNS rebinding attack <https://en."
"wikipedia.org/wiki/DNS_rebinding>`__ against the GUI."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:91
msgid ""
"To pass this test, ensure that you are accessing the GUI using an URL that "
"begins with ``http://localhost``, ``http://127.0.0.1`` or ``http://[::1]``. "
"HTTPS is fine too, of course."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:95
msgid ""
"If you are using a proxy in front of Syncthing you may need to disable this "
"check, after ensuring that the proxy provides sufficient authentication to "
"protect against unauthorized access. Either:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:99
msgid "Make sure the proxy sets a ``Host`` header containing ``localhost``, or"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:100
msgid ""
"Set :stconf:opt:`gui.insecureSkipHostcheck` in the advanced settings, or"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:101
msgid "Bind the GUI/API to a non-localhost listen port."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:103
msgid ""
"In all cases, username/password authentication and HTTPS should be used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:106
msgid "My Syncthing database is corrupt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:108
msgid ""
"This is almost always a result of bad RAM, storage device or other hardware. "
"When the index database is found to be corrupt Syncthing cannot operate and "
"will note this in the logs and exit. To overcome this delete the :ref:"
"`database folder <config-locations>` inside Syncthing's data directory and "
"re-start Syncthing. It will then need to perform a full re-hashing of all "
"shared folders. You should check your system in case the underlying cause is "
"indeed faulty hardware which may put the system at risk of further data loss."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:118
msgid "Why do I see Syncthing twice in task manager?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:120
msgid ""
"One process manages the other, to capture logs and manage restarts. This "
"makes it easier to handle upgrades from within Syncthing itself, and also "
"ensures that we get a nice log file to help us narrow down the cause for "
"crashes and other bugs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:126
msgid "How can I view the history of changes?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:128
msgid ""
"The web GUI contains a ``Recent Changes`` button under the device list which "
"displays changes since the last (re)start of Syncthing. With the ``--audit`` "
"option you can enable a persistent, detailed log of changes and most "
"activities, which contains a JSON-formatted sequence of events in the ``~/."
"local/state/syncthing/audit-_date_-_time_.log`` file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:135
msgid "Does the audit log contain every change?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:137
msgid ""
"The audit log (and the ``Recent Changes`` window) sees the changes that your "
"Syncthing sees. When Syncthing is continuously connected it usually sees "
"every change happening immediately and thus knows which node initiated the "
"change. When topology gets complex or when your node reconnects after some "
"time offline, Syncthing synchronises with its neighbours: It gets the latest "
"synchronised state from the neighbour, which is the *result* of all the "
"changes between the last known state (before disconnect or network delay) "
"and the current state at the neighbour, and if there were updates, deletes, "
"creates, conflicts, which were overlapping we only see the *latest change* "
"for a given file or directory (and the node where that latest change "
"occurred). When we connect to multiple neighbours Syncthing decides which "
"neighbour has the latest state, or if the states conflict it initiates the "
"conflict resolution procedure, which in the end results in a consistent up-"
"to-date state with all the neighbours."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:154
msgid "Why does Syncthing connect to this unknown/suspicious address?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:156
msgid ""
"If you see outgoing connections to odd and unexpected addresses these are "
"most likely connections to :ref:`relay servers <Relaying>`. Relay servers "
"are run by volunteers all over the world. They usually listen on ports 443 "
"or 22067, though this is controlled by the user running it. You can compare "
"the address you are concerned about with `the current list of active relays "
"<https://relays.syncthing.net>`__. Relays do not and can not see the data "
"transmitted via them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:165
msgid "I am seeing the error message \"folder marker missing\". What do I do?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:167
msgid ""
"Syncthing uses a specific marker usually called ``.stfolder`` to determine "
"whether a folder is healthy. This is a safety check to ensure that your "
"folder is properly readable and present on disk. For example, if you remove "
"a USB drive from your computer or unmount a filesystem, then syncthing must "
"know whether you have really deleted **all** of your files. Therefore, "
"syncthing always checks that the ``.stfolder`` is present."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:173
msgid ""
"When this error appears, syncthing assumes that the folder has encountered "
"some type of error and will stop syncing it until the ``.stfolder`` "
"reappears. Once that happens, all changes made to the folder locally will be "
"synced (i.e. missing files will be considered deletions)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:177
msgid ""
"If you get this error message, check the folder in question on your storage. "
"If you have unmounted the folder (or a parent of it), you must remount it "
"for syncthing to resume syncing this folder."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:181
msgid ""
"If you have moved the folder, you must either move it back to its original "
"location, or remove the folder from within the syncthing UI and re-add it at "
"its new location."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:184
msgid ""
"If the folder is present on disk, with all of its children files and "
"directories, but the ``.stfolder`` is still missing:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:187
msgid ""
"It is possible that a file cleaning software has removed the ``.stfolder``. "
"Some software removes empty folders, and the ``.stfolder`` is often empty. "
"This happens particularly often on Android. To remediate, recreate the ``."
"stfolder`` and add a dummy file in it, or add an exception to your cleaning "
"software."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:192
msgid ""
"If you are still unsure what has happened, you can remove the folder from "
"within the syncthing UI and re-add it at the same location. This causes "
"syncthing to attempt an automatic re-creation of the ``.stfolder``. Next, it "
"will also reset the database state of this folder. It will be considered a "
"\"new\" folder, meaning that its files will be merged with files from remote "
"devices."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:197
msgid ""
"Also see the :ref:`marker FAQ <marker-faq>` for more information about the "
"folder marker."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:200
msgid "Why do my Windows computers always connect through a relay?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/troubleshooting.rst:202
msgid ""
"When connecting to a new network, Windows by default sets its profile to "
"\"Public\". While being more secure, this setting commonly prevents "
"applications like Syncthing from being able to establish direct connections "
"with the device in question, forcing it to connect through a relay. If you "
"would rather connect your devices directly, please follow `the official "
"Microsoft instructions on how to change the network profile to \"Private\" "
"<https://support.microsoft.com/windows/essential-network-settings-and-tasks-"
"in-windows-f21a9bbc-c582-55cd-35e0-73431160a1b9#bkmk_network_profile>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:2
msgid "Usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:5
msgid "What if there is a conflict?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:8
msgid ":ref:`conflict-handling`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:13
msgid "How do I serve a folder from a read only filesystem?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:15
msgid ""
"Syncthing requires a \"folder marker\" to indicate that the folder is "
"present and healthy. By default this is a directory called ``.stfolder`` "
"that is created by Syncthing when the folder is added. If this folder can't "
"be created (you are serving files from a CD or something) you can instead "
"set the advanced config ``Marker Name`` to the name of some file or folder "
"that you know will always exist in the folder."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:23
msgid "I really hate the ``.stfolder`` directory, can I remove it?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:25
msgid "See the previous question."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:28
msgid "Am I able to nest shared folders in Syncthing?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:30
msgid ""
"Sharing a folder that is within an already shared folder is possible, but it "
"has its caveats. What you must absolutely avoid are circular shares. This is "
"just one example, there may be other undesired effects. Nesting shared "
"folders is not supported, recommended or coded for, but it can be done "
"successfully when you know what you're doing - you have been warned."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:37
msgid "How do I rename/move a synced folder?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:39
msgid ""
"Syncthing doesn't have a direct way to do this, as it's potentially "
"dangerous to do so if you're not careful - it may result in data loss if "
"something goes wrong during the move and is synchronized to your other "
"devices."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:44
msgid ""
"The easy way to rename or move a synced folder on the local system is to "
"remove the folder in the Syncthing UI, move it on disk, then re-add it using "
"the new path."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:48
msgid ""
"It's important to do this when the folder is already in sync between your "
"devices, as it is otherwise unpredictable which changes will \"win\" after "
"the move. Changes made on other devices may be overwritten, or changes made "
"locally may be overwritten by those on other devices."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:53
msgid ""
"An alternative way is to shut down Syncthing, move the folder on disk "
"(including the ``.stfolder`` marker), edit the path directly in ``config."
"xml`` in the configuration folder (see :doc:`/users/config`) and then start "
"Syncthing again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:58
msgid "How do I configure multiple users on a single machine?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:60
msgid ""
"Each user should run their own Syncthing instance. Be aware that you might "
"need to configure listening ports such that they do not overlap (see :doc:`/"
"users/config`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:64
msgid "Does Syncthing support syncing between folders on the same system?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:66
msgid ""
"No. Syncthing is not designed to sync locally and the overhead involved in "
"doing so using Syncthing's method would be wasteful. There are better "
"programs to achieve this such as `rsync <https://rsync.samba.org/>`__ or "
"`Unison <https://www.cis.upenn.edu/~bcpierce/unison>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:72
msgid ""
"When I do have two distinct Syncthing-managed folders on two hosts, how does "
"Syncthing handle moving files between them?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:74
msgid ""
"Syncthing does not specially handle this case, and most files will most "
"likely get re-downloaded."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:77
msgid ""
"In detail, the behavior depends on the scan order. If you have folders A and "
"B, and move files from A to B, if A gets scanned first, it will announce the "
"removal of the files to others who will then remove the files. As you rescan "
"B, B will announce the addition of new files, and other peers will have "
"nowhere to get them from apart from re-downloading them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:83
msgid ""
"If B gets rescanned first, B will announce additions first, and remote peers "
"will then reconstruct the files (not rename, more like copying block by "
"block) from A, and then as A gets rescanned, it will remove the files from A."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:87
msgid ""
"A workaround would be to copy first from A to B, rescan B, wait for B to "
"copy the files on the remote side, and then delete from A."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:91
msgid "Can I help initial sync by copying files manually?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:93
msgid ""
"If you have a large folder that you want to keep in sync over a not-so-fast "
"network, and you have the possibility to move all files to the remote device "
"in a faster manner, here is a procedure to follow:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:95
msgid ""
"Create the folder on the local device, but don't share it with the remote "
"device yet."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:96
msgid ""
"Copy the files from the local device to the remote device using regular file "
"copy. If this takes a long time (perhaps requiring travelling there "
"physically), it may be a good idea to make sure that the files on the local "
"device are not updated while you are doing this."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:97
msgid ""
"Create the folder on the remote device, and copy the Folder ID from the "
"folder on the local device, as we want the folders to be considered the "
"same. Then wait until scanning the folder is done."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:99
msgid ""
"Now share the folder with the other device, on both sides. Syncthing will "
"exchange file information, updating the database, but existing files will "
"not be transferred. This may still take a while initially, be patient and "
"wait until it settled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:102
msgid "Is Syncthing my ideal backup application?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:104
msgid ""
"No. Syncthing is not a great backup application because all changes to your "
"files (modifications, deletions, etc.) will be propagated to all your "
"devices. You can enable versioning, but we encourage you to use other tools "
"to keep your data safe from your (or our) mistakes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:110
msgid "How can I exclude files with brackets (``[]``) in the name?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:112
msgid ""
"The patterns in .stignore are glob patterns, where brackets are used to "
"denote character ranges. That is, the pattern ``q[abc]x`` will match the "
"files ``qax``, ``qbx`` and ``qcx``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:116
msgid ""
"To match an actual file *called* ``q[abc]x`` the pattern needs to \"escape\" "
"the brackets, like so: ``q\\[abc\\]x``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:119
msgid ""
"On Windows, use the ``|`` character to escape the brackets (``q|[abc|]x``), "
"as the ``\\`` character is used as a path separator."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:123
msgid "How do I access the web GUI from another computer?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:125
msgid ""
"The default listening address is 127.0.0.1:8384, so you can only access the "
"GUI from the same machine.  This is for security reasons.  To access it from "
"another computer, change the ``GUI listen address`` option in the web GUI "
"from ``127.0.0.1:8384`` to ``0.0.0.0:8384``, or change the ``config.xml``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:130
msgid ""
"<gui enabled=\"true\" tls=\"false\">\n"
"  <address>127.0.0.1:8384</address>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:135
msgid "to"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:137
msgid ""
"<gui enabled=\"true\" tls=\"true\">\n"
"  <address>0.0.0.0:8384</address>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:142
msgid ""
"Then the GUI is accessible from everywhere.  There is no filtering based on "
"e.g. source address (use a firewall for that).  You should set a password "
"and enable HTTPS with this configuration.  You can do this from inside the "
"GUI."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:146
msgid ""
"If both your computers are Unix-like (Linux, Mac, etc.) you can also leave "
"the GUI settings at default and use an SSH port forward to access it.  For "
"example,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:149
msgid "$ ssh -L 9090:127.0.0.1:8384 user@othercomputer.example.com"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:153
msgid ""
"will log you into ``othercomputer.example.com``, and present the *remote* "
"Syncthing GUI on http://localhost:9090 on your *local* computer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:156
msgid ""
"If you only want to access the remote GUI and don't want the terminal "
"session, use this example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:159
msgid "$ ssh -N -L 9090:127.0.0.1:8384 user@othercomputer.example.com"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:163
msgid ""
"If only your remote computer is Unix-like, you can still access it with SSH "
"from Windows.  Under Windows 10 or later you can use the same ``ssh`` "
"command if you `install the OpenSSH Client <https://learn.microsoft.com/"
"windows-server/administration/openssh/openssh_install_firstuse>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:168
msgid "I don't like the GUI or the theme. Can it be changed?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:170
msgid ""
"You can change the theme in the settings. Syncthing ships with other themes "
"than the default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:173
msgid ""
"If you want a custom theme or a completely different GUI, you can add your "
"own. By default, Syncthing will look for a directory ``gui`` inside the "
"Syncthing home folder. To change the directory to look for themes, you need "
"to set the STGUIASSETS environment variable. To get the concrete directory, "
"run syncthing with the ``--paths`` parameter. It will print all the relevant "
"paths, including the \"GUI override directory\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:181
msgid ""
"To add e.g. a red theme, you can create the file ``red/assets/css/theme."
"css`` inside the GUI override directory to override the default CSS styles."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:184
msgid ""
"To create a whole new GUI, you should checkout the files at https://github."
"com/syncthing/syncthing/tree/main/gui/default to get an idea how to do that."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:190
msgid "How do I upgrade Syncthing?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:192
msgid ""
"If you use a package manager such as Debian's apt-get, you should upgrade "
"using the package manager. If you use the binary packages linked from "
"Syncthing.net, you can use Syncthing's built-in automatic upgrade "
"functionality."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:196
msgid ""
"If automatic upgrades is enabled (which is the default), Syncthing will "
"upgrade itself automatically within 24 hours of a new release."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:199
msgid ""
"The upgrade button appears in the web GUI when a new version has been "
"released. Pressing it will perform an upgrade."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:202
msgid "To force an upgrade from the command line, run ``syncthing --upgrade``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:204
msgid ""
"Note that your system should have CA certificates installed which allows a "
"secure connection to GitHub (e.g. FreeBSD requires ``sudo pkg install "
"ca_root_nss``). If ``curl`` or ``wget`` works with normal HTTPS sites, then "
"so should Syncthing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:210
msgid "Where do I find the latest release?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:212
msgid ""
"We release new versions through GitHub. The latest release is always found "
"`on the release page <https://github.com/syncthing/syncthing/releases/"
"latest>`_. Unfortunately GitHub does not provide a single URL to "
"automatically download the latest version. We suggest to use the `GitHub API "
"<https://api.github.com/repos/syncthing/syncthing/releases/latest>`__ and "
"parsing the JSON response."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:221
msgid "How do I run Syncthing as a daemon process on Linux?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:223
msgid ""
"If you're using systemd, runit, or upstart, we ship `example configurations "
"<https://github.com/syncthing/syncthing/tree/main/etc>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:225
msgid ""
"If however you're not using one of these tools, you have a couple of "
"options. If your system has a tool called ``start-stop-daemon`` installed "
"(that's the name of the command, not the package), look into the local "
"documentation for that, it will almost certainly cover 100% of what you want "
"to do.  If you don't have ``start-stop-daemon``, there are a bunch of other "
"software packages you could use to do this.  The most well known is called "
"daemontools, and can be found in the standard package repositories for "
"almost every modern Linux distribution. Other popular tools with similar "
"functionality include S6 and the aforementioned runit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:238
msgid ""
"How do I increase the inotify limit to get my filesystem watcher to work?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:240
msgid ""
"You are probably reading this because you encountered the following error "
"with the filesystem watcher on linux:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:243
msgid ""
"Failed to start filesystem watcher for folder yourLabel (yourID): failed to "
"set up inotify handler. Please increase inotify limits, see https://docs."
"syncthing.net/users/faq.html#inotify-limits"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:246
msgid ""
"Linux typically restricts the number of watches per user (usually 8192). If "
"you have many directories, you will need to adjust that number."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:249
msgid "On many Linux distributions you can run the following to fix it::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:251
msgid ""
"echo \"fs.inotify.max_user_watches=204800\" | sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.conf"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:253
msgid ""
"On Arch Linux and potentially others it is preferred to write this line into "
"a separate file, i.e. you should run::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:256
msgid ""
"echo \"fs.inotify.max_user_watches=204800\" | sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.d/90-"
"inotify-max-user-watches.conf"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:258
msgid ""
"This only takes effect after a reboot. To adjust the limit immediately, run::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:260
msgid "echo 204800 | sudo tee /proc/sys/fs/inotify/max_user_watches"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:263
msgid "How do I reset the GUI password?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:265
msgid ""
"If you've forgotten / lost the GUI password, you can reset it using the :"
"option:`--gui-password` (and possibly :option:`--gui-user`) options to the "
"``syncthing generate`` subcommand.  This should be done while Syncthing is "
"not running."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:270
msgid "Stop Syncthing: ``syncthing cli operations shutdown``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:271
msgid ""
"``syncthing generate --gui-password=myNewPassword --gui-user=newUserName``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:272
msgid "Restart Syncthing as usual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:274
msgid ""
"*Alternatively, in step 2*, you can manually delete the :stconf:opt:`<user> "
"<gui.user>` and :stconf:opt:`<password> <gui.password>` XML tags from the "
"``<gui>`` block in file ``config.xml``.  The location of the file depends on "
"the OS and is described in the :doc:`configuration documentation </users/"
"config>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:279
msgid ""
"For example, the two emphasized lines below would be removed from the file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq-parts/usage.rst:281
msgid ""
"<gui enabled=\"true\" tls=\"false\">\n"
"   <address>127.0.0.1:8384</address>\n"
"   <user>syncguy</user>\n"
"   <password>$2a$10$s9wWHOQe...Cq7GPye69</password>\n"
"   <apikey>9RCKohqCAyrj5RjpyZdR2wXmQ9PyQFeN</apikey>\n"
"   <theme>default</theme>\n"
"</gui>"
msgstr ""
